# PostgreSQL log rotation for the jiuyue box.
#
# Installed by deploy/provision.sh to /etc/logrotate.d/jiuyue-postgresql.
#
# On Debian/Ubuntu PostgreSQL writes a plain file via `pg_ctl -l`
# (/var/log/postgresql/postgresql-<ver>-<cluster>.log) and does NOT run its own
# collector, so PostgreSQL's log_rotation_* settings do nothing and rotation is
# logrotate's job. This repository takes ownership of that rotation so the cap is
# an explicit BYTE limit instead of Debian's count-based default:
#
#   size 50M     -> the live file never exceeds ~50 MB
#   rotate 5     -> 5 compressed generations are kept
#   compress     -> generations are gzipped (a chat app's text logs compress ~10x)
#   copytruncate -> the file is copied and truncated IN PLACE; PostgreSQL keeps
#                   writing to the same append-mode fd, so no reload/restart is
#                   needed (this is exactly what Debian's own config does)
#
# Arithmetic: ceiling ~ 50 MB live + 5 compressed generations (typically tens of
# MB total). At the cap logrotate copies the file, truncates it to zero, and
# deletes the oldest generation — `/` does not grow over time.
#
# `provision.sh` moves Debian's /etc/logrotate.d/postgresql-common aside, because
# logrotate refuses to process two entries for the same log path.

/var/log/postgresql/*.log {
	size 50M
	rotate 5
	compress
	delaycompress
	missingok
	notifempty
	copytruncate
	su root root
}
